<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galle is a port city on the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Long a trading harbour, it was fortified by the Portuguese in the late 16th century and captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in 1640, which made it their main base on the island until they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rebuilt its walls, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle-fort">Galle Fort</a> is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galle.jpg" alt="Galle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rovin Shanila · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galle_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Galle lies on the south-western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, 119 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>. In ancient times it was known as Gimhathiththa, thought to mean a port near the River Gin. The Sinhalese name Gālla is believed to come from a word for a place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> are herded, because bullock carts sheltered there after long journeys; another theory derives the name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> gallus or Portuguese galo, meaning rooster, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> used as the symbol of Galle. The hill of Rumassala forms a natural barrier east of the harbour, and local tradition links it with events of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Galle was a prominent seaport long before European rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, Indians and Chinese traded through it. The Moroccan traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who called it Qali, visited in 1342. In the 12th and 13th centuries Sinhalese refugees fleeing Tamil armies from the north settled in and near the town. In 1411 the Galle Trilingual Inscription, in Chinese, Tamil and Persian, was set up to commemorate the second visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> of the Chinese admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>.</p><p>In 1502 a small Portuguese fleet under Lourenço de Almeida, blown off course on its way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, stopped briefly at Galle before going on to the king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte">Kotte</a>. In 1640 the Portuguese surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, whose fort, with a granite wall and three bastions named Sun, Moon and Star, still stands. After the British took over the country from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1796, they kept the fort unchanged and used it as the district's administrative centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Galle is the provincial capital and largest city of Southern Province and the capital of Galle District, with a population mostly Sinhalese and with many Muslims, particularly in the fort area. Its fort is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and the largest remaining fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> built by European occupiers. Other landmarks include the natural harbour, the National Maritime Museum and St Mary's Cathedral. The city was devastated by the tsunami of 26 December 2004. A railway and the Galle Road link it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Berlin Observatory, Johann <b>Galle</b> found Neptune within a degree of where Urbain Le Verrier had calculated.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch took Batticaloa in 1638, Trincomalee in 1639 and <b>Galle</b> in 1640, then Colombo in 1656 and Jaffna in 1658, but kept the conquered coasts for themselves rather than handing them to Kandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch East India Company, allied with Rajasinha II of Kandy, captured <b>Galle</b> in 1640, Colombo in 1656 and Jaffna in 1658, ending Portuguese rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-ceylon">Portuguese Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
